<!-- PORSCHE 911 -->

<h1 style="text-align:center; color:#ff3333; font-size:270%;">
    PORSCHE 911
</h1>

<h2 style="text-align:center; color:#ffffff;">
    THE LEGEND THAT KEPT EVOLVING
</h2>

<p style="text-align:center;">
    <img
    src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1e/Porsche_911_Carrera_4S_%28991%2C_2014%29_%2852924891951%29.jpg"
    width="650">
</p>

<p style="text-align:center; color:#aebbd0;">
    <i>The Porsche 911 — a sports-car design that has evolved for generations.</i>
</p>


<h2 style="color:#ff3333;">
    1. HOW DID THE PORSCHE 911 BEGIN?
</h2>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    The story of the Porsche 911 began in the early 1960s.
    Porsche was looking for a successor to the Porsche 356,
    its successful sports car.
</p>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    Porsche presented a new sports car called the
    <b>Type 901</b> at the International Motor Show in Frankfurt
    on 12 September 1963.
</p>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    Before the car entered production, its name was changed from
    901 to <b>911</b>.
</p>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    The production Porsche 911 appeared in 1964.
    That simple number eventually became one of the most famous
    names in the world of sports cars.
</p>


<h2 style="color:#ff3333;">
    2. THE ORIGINAL 911
</h2>

<p style="text-align:center;">
    <img
    src="https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/wWNJIse49s7oCWFCfnsE9k7bSHuMPyDFOR6nLFN72igAnX7gPfdsHgXCa7A3V01SLt5OBzu8tlRoh4hjlJJm8T4-KSoyYlO-9IoWqr06bNkssyXCgJB9dWxdCoqhIlqNZaX3KBoCwW2NOSjlKv4JxnslTHluGWIO44leItPNbHZVo5QtJOsl2YIca_yLPiaU?purpose=fullsize"
    width="650">
</p>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    The original 911 used a <b>2.0-litre six-cylinder boxer engine</b>.
    The engine was air-cooled and mounted at the rear.
</p>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    It produced about <b>130 PS</b> and could reach approximately
    <b>210 km/h</b>.
</p>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    The rear-engine layout became one of the defining features
    of the Porsche 911.
</p>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    The 911 was also designed to be practical. It had small rear
    seats and luggage space, making it possible to use the car
    beyond a racetrack.
</p>


<h2 style="color:#ff3333;">
    3. WHY DID THE 911 LOOK SO DIFFERENT?
</h2>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    The 911 was designed with a low roofline, rounded headlights,
    a sloping front and a distinctive rear-engine shape.
</p>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    Its proportions were unusual for a sports car, but they became
    part of its identity.
</p>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    The most interesting thing is that Porsche did not completely
    replace the design every few years.
</p>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    Instead, engineers kept improving the car while designers
    continued the recognizable 911 silhouette.
</p>


<h2 style="color:#ff3333;">
    4. THE 911 TARGA
</h2>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    Porsche introduced the <b>911 Targa</b> in the 1960s.
</p>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    It offered an open-air driving experience while retaining
    important structural and safety features.
</p>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    The Targa became another important member of the 911 family
    and developed its own identity.
</p>


<h2 style="color:#ff3333;">
    5. THE CARRERA RS 2.7
</h2>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    The <b>911 Carrera RS 2.7</b> became one of the most famous
    performance versions of the early 911.
</p>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    Engineers concentrated on reducing weight and improving
    performance.
</p>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    It became famous for its distinctive rear spoiler,
    often called the <b>"ducktail"</b>.
</p>

<p style="font-size:120%; color:#e8edf7;">
    The Carrera RS showed that performance is not only about
    increasing engine power. Weight, aerodynamics and handling
    also matter.
</p>

